  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Consider a sphere such that its volume $V$ can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${r}^{\u2032}(V)$ if $r$ is the length of the radius of the sphere?",
  "worked_solution": "The function $r(V)$ gives the length of the radius of a sphere with volume $V.$ So,\n\n$$\n{r}^{\u2032}(V)=\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}V}=\\frac{\\text{Change in\u00a0}r}{\\text{Change in\u00a0}V}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${r}^{\u2032}(V)$ is the rate at which the radius changes as the volume changes.",
